\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The ladder.} (a) Exact-gradient chains: a no-floor, $\gamma $-matched true-pass-rate oracle matches the full stack within the observed five-seed variation --- allocation saturates --- and recycling still adds on top of the oracle. (b) Frontier-heavy regime: allocation scores exactly zero; recycling solves it, and does so equally under uniform sampling (.931 vs.\ .928) --- creation, not allocation, is the live channel. (c) Maze at matched wall-clock: 3/3 seeds on both meters. (d) GSM8K $2{\times }2$, registered run, error bars = measured same-model eval noise (SD .0094): GRPO+teacher is the only cell that regresses (pre-registered P-G2; a replication seed did not reproduce it --- \S  6.7); the teacher's MaxRL effect is a null at this budget (P-G1).}}{8}{figure.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ladder}{{4}{8}{\textbf {The ladder.} (a) Exact-gradient chains: a no-floor, $\gamma $-matched true-pass-rate oracle matches the full stack within the observed five-seed variation --- allocation saturates --- and recycling still adds on top of the oracle. (b) Frontier-heavy regime: allocation scores exactly zero; recycling solves it, and does so equally under uniform sampling (.931 vs.\ .928) --- creation, not allocation, is the live channel. (c) Maze at matched wall-clock: 3/3 seeds on both meters. (d) GSM8K $2{\times }2$, registered run, error bars = measured same-model eval noise (SD .0094): GRPO+teacher is the only cell that regresses (pre-registered P-G2; a replication seed did not reproduce it --- \S 6.7); the teacher's MaxRL effect is a null at this budget (P-G1)}{figure.37}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{6.2 Can any sampler act when nothing is learnable? (frontier-heavy regime, max pool pass rate $10^{-5}$; 5 seeds, artifact committed).}{8}{section*.39}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{6.3 Does the estimator main effect survive real gradients? (maze transformer at matched wall-clock; 1.26M params, 13 levels, ${\sim }30$ runs, 3 seeds).}{9}{section*.40}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Where and when GRPO's coverage goes (maze, level-resolved, matched wall-clock).} \emph  {(a)} $\Delta \text  {pass@}8$ (final $-$ warmstart) by difficulty band: GRPO's loss lives on the easy/mid band (all 4 runs; dots are runs) while its $\text  {pass@}1$ there rises --- sharpening; MaxRL holds the easy band and gains on the frontier band (18 runs; a heterogeneous run inventory --- descriptive, no $p$-value). \emph  {(b)} Diversity premium $\overline  {\text  {pass@}8-\text  {pass@}1}$ vs.\ wall-clock (mean and min--max across runs): GRPO's erosion begins within the first ${\sim }25$--$50$ steps, before any level saturates; MaxRL stabilizes after warmup.}}{10}{figure.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bands}{{5}{10}{\textbf {Where and when GRPO's coverage goes (maze, level-resolved, matched wall-clock).} \emph {(a)} $\Delta \passat {8}$ (final $-$ warmstart) by difficulty band: GRPO's loss lives on the easy/mid band (all 4 runs; dots are runs) while its $\passat {1}$ there rises --- sharpening; MaxRL holds the easy band and gains on the frontier band (18 runs; a heterogeneous run inventory --- descriptive, no $p$-value). \emph {(b)} Diversity premium $\overline {\passat {8}-\passat {1}}$ vs.\ wall-clock (mean and min--max across runs): GRPO's erosion begins within the first ${\sim }25$--$50$ steps, before any level saturates; MaxRL stabilizes after warmup}{figure.43}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{6.4 Where and when does GRPO's coverage go? (level-resolved).}{10}{section*.41}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{6.5 When does allocation \emph  {not} pay? (IsaacLab legged locomotion, pre-registered null).}{10}{section*.44}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {GSM8K $2{\times }2$ (pre-registered; registered run shown; error bars = measured same-model eval noise, SD .0094).} GRPO+teacher is the only cell that regresses in its second half (P-G2 on this run; the load-bearing statistic is the endpoint gap vs.\ plain GRPO, $2$--$4\times $ the repeated-eval noise --- a descriptive signal-to-noise read, not a test; the second-half dip alone is within noise; a replication seed with weaker measured steering climbed instead --- \S  6.7); under MaxRL the identical teacher trains stably to its best value but does not beat uniform at this budget (P-G1: null).}}{11}{figure.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gsm8k}{{6}{11}{\textbf {GSM8K $2{\times }2$ (pre-registered; registered run shown; error bars = measured same-model eval noise, SD .0094).} GRPO+teacher is the only cell that regresses in its second half (P-G2 on this run; the load-bearing statistic is the endpoint gap vs.\ plain GRPO, $2$--$4\times $ the repeated-eval noise --- a descriptive signal-to-noise read, not a test; the second-half dip alone is within noise; a replication seed with weaker measured steering climbed instead --- \S 6.7); under MaxRL the identical teacher trains stably to its best value but does not beat uniform at this budget (P-G1: null)}{figure.47}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{6.6 What does a curriculum add beyond task spread? (Gymnasium control --- binary-success RLVR on classic control; official-task currency).}{11}{section*.45}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{6.7 Does it transfer to LLM scale? (GSM8K $2{\times }2$, pre-registered; SmolLM2-360M, $N{=}16$, one A10G).}{11}{section*.48}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Recycling-induced sharpening and its derived mitigation (Countdown, tier 1).} \emph  {(a)} Three seeds, both axes: recycling (B2) buys mean@16 with $\text  {pass@}16$ coverage. On this tier the moderate gate (B3) gives back most of the mean without moving coverage --- tier-1 destinations saturate early, so admitted relabels are few --- while full-strength gating (corrected decay, 1 seed) returns coverage \emph  {above} baseline at the mean's full price. The settings are consistent with a mean-vs-coverage trade (not yet a monotone dial: the strong point is one seed on different code), and the useful middle lives on the frontier tier, where the same moderate setting restores coverage to baseline ($.279{\pm }.019$ vs.\ $.274$) keeping $\sim $60\% of the mean gain (\S  6.8). \emph  {(b)} The mechanism in-run: the gate's rejection rate rises monotonically as the model's reachable set saturates its relabel destinations, while the ungated dose rides its cap. \emph  {(c)} Gated recycling preserves \emph  {more} policy entropy than no recycling --- admitted relabels are a spread-preserving gradient.}}{12}{figure.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sharpening}{{7}{12}{\textbf {Recycling-induced sharpening and its derived mitigation (Countdown, tier 1).} \emph {(a)} Three seeds, both axes: recycling (B2) buys mean@16 with $\passat {16}$ coverage. On this tier the moderate gate (B3) gives back most of the mean without moving coverage --- tier-1 destinations saturate early, so admitted relabels are few --- while full-strength gating (corrected decay, 1 seed) returns coverage \emph {above} baseline at the mean's full price. The settings are consistent with a mean-vs-coverage trade (not yet a monotone dial: the strong point is one seed on different code), and the useful middle lives on the frontier tier, where the same moderate setting restores coverage to baseline ($.279{\pm }.019$ vs.\ $.274$) keeping $\sim $60\% of the mean gain (\S 6.8). \emph {(b)} The mechanism in-run: the gate's rejection rate rises monotonically as the model's reachable set saturates its relabel destinations, while the ungated dose rides its cap. \emph {(c)} Gated recycling preserves \emph {more} policy entropy than no recycling --- admitted relabels are a spread-preserving gradient}{figure.50}{}}
